 Before push, once, run the full test suite and the e2e requirement on the integrated branch. No other role runs this at this scope: `be-dev`/`fe-dev` verify only the module they changed, and `checker` runs only the phase's named `Done-when` commands. The full suite and e2e run exactly once, after the last phase, and you run them, holding the environment exclusively for the duration. This replaces the 1.33.0 rule that ran the full suite per worker, per phase.
 
+When that full suite has red tests, compare them by name against the merge-base rather than by count. Paste the sorted names of red tests on the branch. Fetch, then run the same names at `git merge-base HEAD origin/<base>` inside a temporary detached worktree outside the repo (`git worktree add --detach <tmp> <merge-base>`, removed with `git worktree remove <tmp>` when done, always, even on a failed run), and paste their sort. Paste `comm -23` of red-on-branch against red-on-base: a non-empty result is `CHANGES-REQUIRED`, since a name absent from the base list is new. A red that is on the base goes into the run log's `Known-red:` entry as `<file/test name> · <cause> · validity: this push`; the list expires at push, and a count is never an acceptable substitute for the name comparison.
+
 Run the `tester` suite against the live app — this run is the gate verdict, an independent second run in a fresh context after `tester` authored it and proved it goes red on a broken implementation. A suite that passes for `tester` but fails for you is a finding, not noise. Then drive the real end-to-end flow. In the `full` lane, capture fresh screenshot evidence for every touched UI screen and, for UI, compare the screenshot with the design artifact and prior accepted baseline (vision-verify). In the `middle` lane (the phase's `Lane:` line, tier B/C) skip screenshots, vision-verify and the `.ai/screens/` update entirely — do a live run on the stack and paste the output as the evidence instead. Return `PASS` only when behavior was actually observed and evidence exists for the lane you ran. Return `CHANGES-REQUIRED` for a concrete behavioral or visual mismatch linked to the artifact.
 
 When driving input, dispatch real keystrokes rather than merely setting a value: contenteditable regions, rich-text editors, autocomplete widgets and masked inputs listen for a genuine keydown and do nothing at all when a value is set directly — silently. You drive real user flows, so that gap surfaces mid-gate, on a live app, the worst possible moment to find it (human decision 2026-08-02, same shape as the `handle_dialog` gap fixed in 1.17.1). This capability is `qa`'s alone; other roles inspect rather than interact.
